The `inventoryComplete` function is vulnerable to silent passes if the `inventory` object is empty or missing expected keys (such as `reveal_payload_schema` or `question_source_inventory`). Because it only iterates over the values of the present keys using `Object.values`, any omitted keys will not be checked, and `every` will vacuously evaluate to `true`. To prevent this, explicitly verify that all expected inventory keys are present and valid.

```javascript
function inventoryComplete(inventory) {
  const expectedKeys = [
    'amendment_boundary',
    'reveal_payload_schema',
    'question_source_inventory',
    'page_source_reveal_assets',
    'reveal_gate_policy',
    'display_policy',
    'page_first_crop_candidates',
    'crop_quality_gate',
    'self_assessment_copilot_boundary',
    'frontend_fixtures',
    'local_rehearsal',
    'frontend_handoff',
    'prior_1427_1439_preserved_gates'
  ];
  const inv = (inventory && typeof inventory === 'object') ? inventory : {};
  return expectedKeys.every((key) => {
    const entry = inv[key];
    return entry && typeof entry === 'object' &&
      Array.isArray(entry.paths) &&
      entry.paths.length > 0 &&
      entry.paths.every((item) => item?.exists === true && /^[a-f0-9]{64}$/u.test(String(item.sha256)));
  });
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When validating lists of structured objects (such as artifacts), avoid relying solely on array length checks. Instead, explicitly validate that all expected roles or keys are present, and use defensive optional chaining to handle potentially null or malformed entries safely.
</details>

![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The `liveIssueChainClosedAndMerged` function is vulnerable to silent passes if the `issues` array is empty or missing expected elements. Because it filters the input array and then checks `every`, an empty array or an array missing expected issues (1453 through 1464) will result in a vacuous `true` evaluation. To prevent this, explicitly verify that all expected issue numbers are present and closed/merged, and ensure the function fails closed if the input is not an array.

```javascript
function liveIssueChainClosedAndMerged(issues) {
  const isArr = Array.isArray(issues);
  const issuesList = isArr ? issues : [];
  const issueMap = new Map(issuesList.map((i) => [i?.issue, i]));
  const expected = [1453, 1454, 1455, 1456, 1457, 1458, 1459, 1460, 1461, 1462, 1463, 1464];
  return isArr && expected.every((num) => {
    const issue = issueMap.get(num);
    return issue && issue.state === 'CLOSED' && issue.pr_state === 'MERGED';
  });
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When validating expected array fields from upstream schemas or input data, explicitly verify that all expected elements are present (rather than just checking the array length) to prevent silent mismatches from renamed or replaced fields. If the field is present but is not an array, process it as an empty array and trigger a validation failure to ensure the system fails closed.
</details>
